WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.760
A signal outside a cell needs a reader inside.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.800
Where does Reelin come from, and what reads it?

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.620
In 1995, D'Arcangelo and Curran cloned the gene broken in reeler.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.380
It encodes a very large protein, Reelin.

5
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:23.923
That year, Ogawa, Mikoshiba and colleagues made an antibody, CR-50, that stained

6
00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.143
Cajal-Retzius cells.

7
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:30.363
In culture, it made normal cortical tissue organize like reeler tissue.

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.823
It recognizes Reelin, a secreted protein.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:39.103
So Reelin is made at the top of the cortex, where each new wave should stop.

10
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:45.123
Two more mutants, scrambler and yotari, looked like reeler, but had a normal Reelin

11
00:00:45.123 --> 00:00:46.123
gene.

12
00:00:46.383 --> 00:00:49.703
Both had mutations in Dab1, an adaptor protein.

13
00:00:50.463 --> 00:00:52.623
A Dab1 knockout gave the same picture.

14
00:00:53.667 --> 00:01:00.467
Joachim Herz's laboratory removed two lipoprotein receptors, ApoER2 and VLDLR.

15
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.020
Without both, the brain was close to reeler.

16
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.540
Without one, the defect was milder.

17
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:09.463
Think of a relay race.

18
00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:12.483
Remove a runner, and the baton never arrives.

19
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:17.617
But the receptor leg has two runners, with partly overlapping jobs.

20
00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:20.637
Lose one, and some signal still gets through.

21
00:01:21.517 --> 00:01:23.577
Lose both, and the brain is close to reeler.

22
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.190
Matching mutants suggest one relay.

23
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.150
They do not give its order.

24
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:35.173
Predict. Without Reelin, is there more Dab1, or less?

25
00:01:35.993 --> 00:01:37.973
And is more of it phosphorylated, or less?

26
00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:47.773
There is more Dab1 than normal, but less of it is phosphorylated.

27
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:54.577
That shows Reelin affects Dab1, but not yet which acts first.

28
00:01:55.457 --> 00:01:57.137
So add Reelin to cultured neurons.

29
00:01:58.257 --> 00:02:00.237
Dab1 is phosphorylated within minutes.

30
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.650
Reelin binds both receptors directly.

31
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.650
Then the kinases Fyn and Src add phosphate to Dab1.

32
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:14.330
One explanation for the build-up: phosphorylated Dab1 is destroyed through Cullin 5,

33
00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.110
and without Reelin less of it is phosphorylated.

34
00:02:19.683 --> 00:02:24.463
Knock down Cullin 5 in migrating neurons, and active Dab1 builds up.

35
00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:26.723
The neurons migrate too far.

36
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:32.937
So normal positioning needs both the signal and its shut-off.

37
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.600
Takeaway: matching mutants suggest one relay.

38
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.240
Adding Reelin and watching Dab1 respond puts Reelin upstream of Dab1; the binding

39
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.340
studies place the receptors between them.
